WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.400 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Applause]  2012 was a life-changing year for me in  May I got pregnant and then I found out  I was having the high-risk pregnancy  requiring me to be on bed rest for 16  hours a day  on my side so to kill time and entertain  myself  I decided to go on amazon.com and buy  all the highly rated parenting books the  charge reading them the more I read the  more confident I got about my ability to  take on this new challenge I knew it was  going to be hard but in typical V DITA  fashion I had figured out everything  that could potentially go wrong and then  come up with mitigation strategies to  address those challenges so here I was  ready to take this on and then Kim Zara  a very happy and healthy baby four and a  half pounds seventeen and a half inches  long with a vocal capacity of 88  decibels those first few moments were so  precious the first few years  not so much I went back to taking a  full-time job which was very stressful  my husband Sandeep was trying to start a  new business so he also was working  really long hours we hired a full-time  nanny to take care of Zara Zara was  spending a lot of time with the nanny so  not surprisingly Zara got really  attached to the nanny and this really  bothered me but then I remembered in one  of the books that I had read Sheryl  Sandberg in her book lean in talked  about being in the exact same  predicament that I was in so I was like  hey Cheryl went through this you're  going through this it's really no big  deal just don't sweat it  but that advice was really hard for me  to hold on to  the evenings got progressively harder  and the weekends were even worse much of  what I had read in those books was just  not working for me on a daily basis and  it didn't take me long to figure out  there was somebody else in the house who  had it all figured out sumeet knew  exactly what to say how to say it what  to do when to do it how to do it he  essentially had the motherly instinct  that was supposed to be mine  it was my precious gift for carrying our  child for nine months and for the first  time in my life I was jealous of Sandeep  how could it be that I was so successful  at work getting stellar performance  reviews and at home I couldn't even put  Legos together it really frustrated me  to see that and I carried this  frustration into work every day so I  distinctly remember that day in June  2015 I was in I was basically running  from meeting to meeting trying to cope  with everything that was being thrown at  me that day and it was Dora's wellness  appointment at 2:00 p.m. that day and I  didn't want to be late but despite my  best intentions I was late so I showed  up the appointment went really well  Xaro was thriving as a toddler and then  when the appointment was over Sandeep  took Zara back to the car while I waited  there in the office in this really tiny  space probably ten feet by five feet  waiting for my pediatrician to print her  growth charts so here I was standing  there very lost in my thoughts and just  staring at the ground and she looked at  me and she said  so how's it going I have no idea what it  was about those words but the next thing  I knew I was crying and this was no  gentle cry this was really loud and ugly  with lots of Kleenex smudged eyeliner of  the mass and I don't know what it was it  was there was nothing special about what  she said but it was as if she knew  exactly what was going on in my mind she  probably seen this before but for me it  was a first and I was standing there  feeling really lost and completely  defeated by motherhood it took me  several days to process what had  occurred and I think I finally figured  it out from the day I got pregnant and  possibly even before I conceived I had  this image of this perfect mom this mom  who's on time for all her appointments  her house looks really clean the sink is  not piled with dirty dishes the laundry  is completely taken care of  tucked away in drawers she organizes  playdates for her daughter she reads to  her daughter probably in more than two  languages and she's on top of it and I  was miles away from achieving any of  this on a consistent basis and this  really made bothered me it made me  frustrated and angry which made me  Restless in my own skin and my daughter  could feel that which is why she was  running away from me making me feel  worse and this cycle just continued so I  have spent a lot of time thinking about  this and many whiteboard sessions later  I think I finally cracked the code on  this internal dichotomy of a personality  trait that it's so engrained in me so on  one hand this continuous strive for  perfection  please to stress anxiety it lowers my  confidence levels  it makes me unsure of myself and in many  ways it really increases my desire to be  accepted in this world it's what made  those first few years with Zara really  hard but on the other hand it's de  strive for perfection that results in  beautiful products whether that is at  work or at home in the kitchen you can  ask my husband about every meal that I  cook it is done in the absolute hard way  there are no shortcuts but I think what  it results in is a perfect meal or if  he's perfect from my standards and it  makes me happy so and I really cannot  forget that it is that perfection that  got me to the GSB  this dichotomy is really tense but this  tension is beautiful it is this even  today  what does not exist today though is this  constant agony to try to fight it  because what I've realized is that at my  core I'm a perfectionist  I just cannot change that what I can  change is how and when I apply it to my  life  and because essentially what I don't do  anymore is try to apply this  perfectionism to relationships because  relationships are not like recipes two  tablespoons of love and three teaspoons  of compassion is not equal a happy baby  letting my guard down to really be  myself and who I am is what makes me the  mom I want to be and it is this  imperfect mom that Zahra loves thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

